<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Cornwallis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1713">1713</a>–<a href="/years/1776">1776</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Cornwallis was a British army officer and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> from 1749 to 1752. In 1749 he founded Halifax as a naval base and new capital with about 2,500 settlers. During conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a> in Father Le Loutre's War he offered bounties for Mi'kmaq scalps, which has made him a controversial figure. He later governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-cornwallis.jpg" alt="Edward Cornwallis" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joshua Reynolds · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EdwardCornwallisArtGalleryofNovaScotia1756.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cornwallis was the son of Charles, 4th Baron Cornwallis, and Lady Charlotte Butler, daughter of Richard Butler, 1st Earl of Arran. He and his twin brother Frederick Cornwallis became royal pages at 12 and went to Eton at 14. When a fall paralysed Frederick's arm, it was settled that he would enter the church and Edward the army, and in 1731 Edward was commissioned into the 47th Regiment of Foot.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fontenoy">Battle of Fontenoy</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a>, he took command of his regiment when Colonel Craig was killed and organised its retreat; it lost eight officers and 385 men. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-culloden">Battle of Culloden</a> he led 320 men into the Highlands under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-king-of-hanover">Duke of Cumberland</a>'s orders to plunder, burn and destroy Lochaber. His men drove off livestock and destroyed crops and food stores, and some, against his orders, raped and murdered non-combatants.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> he sited the town halfway up the harbour below the hill later called Citadel Hill, and named it after Lord Halifax, President of the Board of Trade. His administration set up an Executive and Legislative Council and the first British law courts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. Mi'kmaq leaders wrote to him in September 1749 that the land belonged to them. After the bounties, settlers brought in only one scalp in nine months, and in May 1751 the Mi'kmaq made their largest attack, the Raid on Dartmouth. He left the colony in October 1752.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1756 he sat on Admiral John Byng's council of war that left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a> to its fate and was court-martialled with the other officers, and in 1757 he voted to retreat at Rochefort. Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> from 14 June 1761, he died in January 1776 and was buried at Culford. Commemorations of him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> have since been contested: after Mi'kmaw protests Halifax removed his statue on 30 January 2018, and streets, a school, a church and Cornwallis Park have been renamed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward Cornwallis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1749 <b>Edward Cornwallis</b> founded Halifax as a naval base and new capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-nova-scotia">Colony of Nova Scotia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1753" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Edward Cornwallis"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 5724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 5724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1753">What was here in 1753? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
